WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.086
Industrial threat hunting is the proactive, defensive search for suspicious or unexplained activity in OT and ICS environments.

00:00:08.086 --> 00:00:12.025
Hunters do not wait for a confirmed alert.

00:00:12.025 --> 00:00:22.598
They begin with a focused question, examine available evidence, and determine whether the environment contains activity that existing monitoring missed or did not explain.

00:00:22.598 --> 00:00:26.123
Alert response starts with a generated signal.

00:00:26.123 --> 00:00:36.282
Hunting starts with a hypothesis, such as whether remote support reached an unexpected controller or whether engineering activity occurred outside an approved window.

00:00:36.282 --> 00:00:43.538
A hunt may discover a security event, an operational fault, a policy gap, or incomplete telemetry.

00:00:43.538 --> 00:00:50.379
OT hunting differs from ordinary enterprise hunting because technical evidence has physical and operational meaning.

00:00:50.379 --> 00:00:55.563
A communication change may affect production, safety, reliability, or process integrity.

00:00:55.563 --> 00:01:01.990
The same behavior can be legitimate maintenance in one context and concerning in another.

00:01:01.990 --> 00:01:05.099
Hunts should be careful and evidence-driven.

00:01:05.099 --> 00:01:09.039
Use existing logs and passive telemetry where possible.

00:01:09.039 --> 00:01:13.807
Define scope, data sources, time range, owners, and stop conditions.

00:01:13.807 --> 00:01:21.063
Do not send unplanned probes, commands, or configuration changes to sensitive systems merely to create evidence.

00:01:21.063 --> 00:01:24.173
Operators and engineers are analytical partners.

00:01:24.173 --> 00:01:28.942
They explain process modes, maintenance, equipment behavior, and safe escalation.

00:01:28.942 --> 00:01:32.881
Security hunters contribute investigative structure and cross-source correlation.

00:01:32.881 --> 00:01:38.478
Together they can distinguish unexplained activity from known work or process failure.

00:01:38.478 --> 00:01:43.247
The goal is not disruption or proving a dramatic theory.

00:01:43.247 --> 00:01:47.601
It is better visibility, earlier detection, and safer decisions.

00:01:47.601 --> 00:01:58.174
A successful hunt can confirm the hypothesis, disprove it, identify a coverage gap, improve inventory, or produce a new detection that protects future operations.
